From fc2f975b7bfe2f52dadc88d0ab49df012ff0b962 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 5 Aug 2026 08:35:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Count where the ++/-- step's operands live, item 3-1

Item 3-1's re-specification named this as the count to take before a
typed backing store is built, on a stated disjunction: if the operand is
an element or a field the step shares that mechanism, and if it is a
local the analysis merely failed to type, it is a much smaller change
aimed somewhere else. It is the second, and not marginally.

The compiler already knows where the operand lives -- it emits a
different branch for each -- so the census carries that into the step as
a compile-time constant. The rows are recorded by an overload while the
total stays with Increment itself, so the rows sum to UnaryUpdate by
construction and a call site the emitter forgot shows up as a shortfall
rather than vanishing. Every suite balances.

Of 17 282 144 steps across the Octane corpus: Element 0, Property 0.3%,
LocalCell 0.0%, LocalSlot 98.1%, GlobalOrWith 1.6%, Other 0. Not one
increment on the corpus is on an array element. Weighted by each suite's
request-to-allocation ratio that is about 7.05 M real boxes, 22.6% of
the 31.16 M the corpus still allocates, and 6.76 M of it is
NavierStokes' alone.

Reading the source names the cascade: the hot updates are ++currentRow
in lin_solve, advect and project, where currentRow = j * rowSize and
rowSize is a FluidField-scope var written from a sibling closure. The
analysis cannot type rowSize, so it cannot type currentRow, so every
++currentRow boxes. One closure variable costs 6.76 M boxes.

The first version of this census reported 98.1% in Other, because an
identifier that resolves statically but is neither a numeric local nor a
JSVariable cell falls through to the shared member-update tail
unlabelled. A residue that size is a claim about the census and not
about the engine, so LocalSlot was split out and Other is now asserted
at zero rather than merely reported.

UpdateTargetCensusTests -- 11 fixtures. Each kind reachable and landing
in its own row; a computed key that is really a string still counting as
an element, so the syntactic limit of the reading is pinned rather than
described; the rows summing to the total; the census counting nothing
when disabled; the answers unchanged with it on. The discriminating one
is that a numeric local contributes nothing at all -- i++ on a raw
double is a native add that never reaches Increment -- without which
"98.1% are locals the analysis did not type" would be consistent with
the census simply counting every local. Three fixtures were wrong twice
before they were right, first assuming a top-level var is a local and
then that it is a global; it is a JSVariable cell, and that is now its
own test.

Counters off by default, no behaviour change.
